nodecode-makeirl is a rewrite of MelMayssonOwen's MelMayssonOwen/hermes-plugins:
https://github.com/MelMayssonOwen/hermes-plugins
commit f203c3f897784d731ef48bc0974104a4d08cf5f8
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Opsibyte (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the package's hosted MCP server, spoken to directly from
Common Lisp -- one JSON-RPC POST per exchange to
https://makeirl.com/api/mcp, the initialize handshake, a
notifications/initialized, an Mcp-Session-Id kept until the server forgets
it, tools/list and tools/call, a JSON body or a server-sent frame read the
same way, and the bearer token in the Authorization header. The package's
tool list, which comes from the server at run time, became two tools of one
Nodecode cell, makeirl_tools and makeirl_call, instead of a fixed set; the
release-gate skill under skills/ became (help :makeirl), in the same order it
was written (upload, start, poll, findings, fixes, artifacts and payment, a
drafted board is not a released one), with its warning that a report checks
rules and does not prove a circuit. A /makeirl command was added for the
operator. The token comes from the cell's own section: written inline, or
named by an environment variable, or held by a 0600 file, where the package
left it to the harness to store the OAuth sign-in.

What was left out: the Agent Plugins v1 manifest (plugin.json, mcp.json)
with its install command and OAuth sign-in -- a Nodecode section is a cell's
config, so a token pasted into it, or named by an environment variable or a
file, replaces the tokens the harness stored; and the package's "untrusted"
trust setting, which made the harness ask before a write-capable call.
Nodecode asks nothing of a tool a model calls, so one makeirl_call can start
a run or create a checkout link, and (help :makeirl) says which call does
what.
